<GuideArticle slug="us-preclearance-aruba-airport"
  title="US preclearance in Aruba, and why it matters"
  metaTitle="US Preclearance at Aruba Airport (AUA) — How It Works & Why It Matters"
  description="Aruba's Queen Beatrix Airport has US customs preclearance. Clear US immigration in Aruba and land home as a domestic passenger. How it works and how early to arrive."
  kicker="Travel · flying home" faq={faq}>
  <p>Here's one of the most useful facts about Aruba, and almost nobody mentions it: <strong>Queen Beatrix International Airport has US preclearance.</strong> If you're flying to the United States, you clear US immigration and customs in Aruba, before you board.</p>
  <h2>Why that's a big deal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You land as a domestic passenger.</strong> Your flight arrives at a domestic gate. There's no customs hall and no immigration line after a long travel day.</li>
    <li><strong>Connections get easier.</strong> Because you've already cleared, a connecting flight inside the US is treated like any domestic connection.</li>
    <li><strong>It's rare.</strong> Only a handful of airports outside North America offer US preclearance. In the Caribbean it is one of Aruba's quiet advantages over most competing islands.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>How it works on departure day</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check in with your airline and drop your bags.</li>
    <li>Complete Aruban departure formalities and security.</li>
    <li>Go through US Customs and Border Protection: passport control and customs, exactly as you would on arrival in the US.</li>
    <li>Board from a sterile US-bound area.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The process is thorough, so give yourself about <strong>three hours</strong> before a US-bound departure. From Casa Playa the airport is about 5 km away, a 10-minute drive.</p>
</GuideArticle>
